<DocsLayout
  title="Quickstart"
  subtitle="From a clean checkout to a Tauri window. Every command is run at the repository root."
  description="Install CADCAMFUN with Bun, run tests, generate and apply Drizzle migrations, and start the desktop dev window."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="before" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Before you install</h2>
    <p>
      You need Bun and a Rust toolchain. The repo root <code>package.json</code> sets
      <code>packageManager</code> to <code>bun@1.4.2</code>. <code>specs/00-overview.md</code> names
      Rust 1.96 as the installed toolchain. The Tauri CLI is not installed with Cargo. It is the
      devDependency <code>@tauri-apps/cli</code> on <code>@cadcamfun/desktop</code>, and
      <code>bun install</code> at the root brings it in.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Check</th><th>Command</th><th>You are looking for</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Bun</td>
          <td><code>bun --version</code></td>
          <td>A Bun that can install this lockfile. The pin in package.json is 1.4.2.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Rust</td>
          <td><code>rustc --version</code></td>
          <td>A toolchain that can compile <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri</code>. The overview names 1.96.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Host triple</td>
          <td><code>rustc -vV</code></td>
          <td>The <code>host:</code> line. The sidecar binary is named <code>sidecar-&lt;that-triple&gt;</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout title="Do not cargo-install Tauri" type="warn">
      <p>
        <code>cargo install tauri-cli</code> is the wrong CLI for this repo. <code>desktop:dev</code>
        calls <code>tauri</code> through the desktop package. A globally installed CLI will not match
        <code>@tauri-apps/cli</code> ^2 that <code>apps/desktop/package.json</code> declares.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      Clone the repo and <code>cd</code> to its root (the directory that contains the root
      <code>package.json</code>, <code>packages/</code>, <code>apps/</code>, and <code>specs/</code>).
      Do not run <code>bun install</code> inside a single package. Workspaces are declared only at
      the root: <code>"workspaces": ["packages/*", "apps/*"]</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Install and test</h2>
    <p>
      Bun is the package manager, the test runner, and the sidecar runtime. Effect is pinned to
      <code>4.0.0-rc.112</code> in every package that depends on it, because
      <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk-next</code> requires that release. One copy of Effect keeps
      <code>Context</code> service tags identical across packages.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From the repo root">
{`bun install
bun test packages apps
bunx tsc -p packages/core/tsconfig.json
bunx tsc -p apps/sidecar/tsconfig.json`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>bun test packages apps</code> is the README suite: every <code>*.test.ts</code> under
      <code>packages/</code> and <code>apps/</code>. The root script <code>bun run test</code> is
      wider. It runs <code>bun test packages apps scripts</code>, so it also runs the tests under
      <code>scripts/</code>. Use the README command for the first pass. Use <code>bun run test</code>
      before you touch the release scripts.
    </p>
    <p>
      The README typecheck takes one tsconfig. There is no package named <code>&lt;name&gt;</code>.
      Use the directory you changed:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>You changed</th><th>Typecheck</th><th>Tests</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>packages/db</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p packages/db/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test packages/db</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>packages/core</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p packages/core/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test packages/core</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>packages/api</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p packages/api/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test packages/api</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>packages/ai</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p packages/ai/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test packages/ai</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>apps/sidecar</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p apps/sidecar/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test apps/sidecar</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>apps/desktop</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p apps/desktop/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test apps/desktop</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>apps/mobile</code></td>
          <td><code>bunx tsc -p apps/mobile/tsconfig.json</code></td>
          <td><code>bun test apps/mobile</code></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>tsconfig.base.json</code> is strict, with <code>noUncheckedIndexedAccess</code> and
      <code>allowImportingTsExtensions</code>. The definition of done in
      <code>specs/00-overview.md</code> §5 is: <code>bun install</code> succeeds, that
      <code>tsc -p</code> exits 0, and <code>bun test &lt;package dir&gt;</code> passes. Most
      package <code>typecheck</code> scripts call <code>tsgo -p tsconfig.json --noEmit</code> rather
      than <code>tsc</code>. <code>bun run typecheck</code> runs those scripts through Turbo, then
      <code>tsgo -p tsconfig.json --noEmit</code> at the root. Mobile and this docs site
      (<code>@cadcamfun/web</code>) use <code>tsc</code> in their own scripts.
    </p>
    <DocCallout title="A failing install is a root-workspace problem" type="note">
      <p>
        If <code>bun install</code> fails, stay at the repo root. Do not delete
        <code>bun.lock</code> and regenerate it to “fix” a pin. Effect must stay
        <code>4.0.0-rc.112</code>. The only shared files an integrating change may edit are the root
        <code>package.json</code> workspaces, <code>tsconfig.base.json</code>, and
        <code>bun.lock</code>. Adding a new package’s own <code>package.json</code> is allowed.
        Editing those shared files is not part of a normal quickstart.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="migrate" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Migrate</h2>
    <p>
      Schema changes belong to <code>packages/db</code> (WP0). Other packages do not talk to SQLite.
      You only generate a migration when <code>packages/db/src/schema.ts</code> changed. On a fresh
      clone you apply the SQL that is already committed.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From the repo root">
{`bun run db:generate
bun run db:migrate`}
    </DocCode>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th></th><th><code>db:generate</code></th><th><code>db:migrate</code></th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Root script</td>
          <td><code>bun run --filter @cadcamfun/db generate</code></td>
          <td><code>bun run --filter @cadcamfun/db migrate</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Package script</td>
          <td><code>drizzle-kit generate</code></td>
          <td><code>bun src/migrate.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Config / entry</td>
          <td><code>packages/db/drizzle.config.ts</code></td>
          <td><code>packages/db/src/migrate.ts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reads</td>
          <td><code>packages/db/src/schema.ts</code> and the existing snapshot in <code>packages/db/drizzle/</code></td>
          <td>The SQL folders under <code>packages/db/drizzle/</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Writes</td>
          <td>A new folder of generated SQL. Do not hand-edit it.</td>
          <td>The SQLite file named below. Creates the parent directory.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      <code>drizzle.config.ts</code> sets <code>dialect: "sqlite"</code>,
      <code>schema: "./src/schema.ts"</code>, <code>out: "./drizzle"</code>, and a credential URL of
      <code>file:./data/cadcamfun.db</code> unless <code>DATABASE_URL</code> is already set in the
      environment. <code>migrate.ts</code> uses the same default after stripping a <code>file:</code>
      prefix: <code>./data/cadcamfun.db</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Bun runs a <code>--filter</code> script with the package directory as the working directory.
      The root command <code>bun run db:migrate</code> therefore resolves that relative path from
      <code>packages/db</code>, which is <code>packages/db/data/cadcamfun.db</code>. README and
      <code>specs/02-database.md</code> write the default as <code>./data/cadcamfun.db</code>. The
      script logs <code>migrated</code> plus the path it opened. That log line is the file you have.
      If your shell already exports <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, unset it when you want this default.
      Do not create a <code>.env</code> file to hold it, and do not open <code>.env*</code> files to
      discover a path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Committed migration folders today (each contains <code>migration.sql</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>packages/db/drizzle/20261004223029_careful_monster_badoon/</code></li>
      <li><code>packages/db/drizzle/20261008030518_redundant_dagger/</code></li>
      <li><code>packages/db/drizzle/20261009012351_moaning_loki/</code></li>
      <li><code>packages/db/drizzle/20261009171602_brown_mad_thinker/</code></li>
    </ul>
    <DocCallout title="This file is not the window’s database" type="warn">
      <p>
        <code>bun run db:migrate</code> updates the CLI file above. The Tauri window’s sidecar opens
        <code>$CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR/cadcamfun.db</code>. The shell sets <code>CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR</code>
        to Tauri’s <code>app_data_dir()</code> (<code>apps/desktop/src-tauri/src/lib.rs</code>) and
        applies the bundled migrations itself on startup. Migrating the CLI file does not migrate
        the running app. Details are on <a href="/docs/guides/local-dev">Local development</a>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      To change the schema: edit <code>packages/db/src/schema.ts</code>, run
      <code>bun run db:generate</code>, and keep the generated SQL. Table and column names stay
      identical to the legacy Prisma SQLite database. Migration SQL must not contain transaction
      control or non-transactional statements such as <code>VACUUM</code>
      (<code>specs/02-database.md</code>). Commit the new folder only when you were asked to commit.
      Database tests do not use this file. They open <code>:memory:</code>, as in
      <code>packages/db/src/database.test.ts</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="desktop" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Desktop window</h2>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="From the repo root">
{`bun run desktop:dev`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The root script is <code>bun run build:sidecar &amp;&amp; bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop tauri dev</code>.
      It always compiles the real sidecar before Tauri starts. Tauri’s
      <code>beforeDevCommand</code> in <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json</code> is
      <code>bun run dev</code>, which is Vite. <code>devUrl</code> is
      <code>http://localhost:1420</code>. The window is 1440×900, minimum 960×600, title
      “CADCAM FUN”, identifier <code>com.cadcamfun.app</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Step inside <code>build:sidecar</code></th><th>Result</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>bun scripts/build.ts --only=compile,resources</code></td>
          <td>Two of the six pipeline steps, in that order.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>compile</td>
          <td>Bun-compiles <code>apps/sidecar/src/main.ts</code> to <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri/binaries/sidecar-&lt;triple&gt;</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>resources</td>
          <td>Copies <code>packages/db/drizzle/</code> to <code>apps/desktop/src-tauri/resources/migrations/</code>.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The shell then spawns that binary, generates a token, and sets
      <code>CADCAMFUN_TOKEN</code>, <code>CADCAMFUN_DATA_DIR</code>, and
      <code>CADCAMFUN_MIGRATIONS_DIR</code>. The sidecar listens on <code>127.0.0.1</code> with port
      <code>0</code> (the OS picks the port). When <code>GET /api/health</code> succeeds it prints
      one JSON line on stdout. Stdout is reserved for that ready event. Effect logs go to stderr.
      Do not add other <code>console.log</code> calls on the sidecar’s stdout.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ready line is one JSON object,
      <code>&#123;&quot;event&quot;:&quot;ready&quot;,&quot;port&quot;:N&#125;</code>,
      where <code>N</code> is the port the OS assigned. The shell waits for that line
      (<code>specs/07-tauri-shell.md</code>, timeout 15 seconds).
    </p>
    <DocCallout title="Do not replace the binary with the stub" type="warn">
      <p>
        <code>apps/desktop/scripts/build-stub-sidecar.ts</code> writes the same
        <code>binaries/sidecar-&lt;triple&gt;</code> path. Without <code>--if-missing</code> it
        overwrites a real sidecar you just compiled. The desktop script
        <code>tauri:dev</code> is <code>bun scripts/build-stub-sidecar.ts --if-missing &amp;&amp; tauri dev</code>.
        That is the shell-only loop (WP5 was verified with a stub). For the real HttpApi, use
        <code>bun run desktop:dev</code> from the root, which runs <code>build:sidecar</code> first.
        <code>--if-missing</code> leaves an existing binary in place, including a real one.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <p>
      Packaged builds, still from the root:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Build scripts in scripts/build.ts and the desktop package">
{`bun run build:sidecar
bun run build:desktop
bun run build
bun scripts/build.ts --help`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>bun run build:desktop</code> is <code>vite build</code> for <code>@cadcamfun/desktop</code>.
      It produces webview assets. It does not produce a <code>.app</code> or a sidecar.
      <code>bun run build</code> is the full pipeline in <code>scripts/build.ts</code>, always in
      this order: install (<code>bun install --frozen-lockfile</code>), check
      (<code>bun run typecheck</code>), compile, resources, bundle (Vite, then
      <code>tauri build</code>), artifacts (SHA-256 and notes). The script does not read
      <code>.env*</code> files. A non-zero exit stops the run. Subsets:
      <code>--only=compile,resources</code> is what <code>build:sidecar</code> already passes;
      <code>--only=notes --dry-run</code> and <code>--target=aarch64-apple-darwin</code> are the
      examples in the script header.
    </p>
    <p>
      CI workflows that call this pipeline live in <code>.github/workflows/</code>:
      <code>ci.yml</code>, <code>build-platforms.yml</code>, <code>release.yml</code>, and
      <code>ios.yml</code>. WP10’s status in the overview is “pipeline + CI + updater; release
      secrets unset”. Do not invent signing keys to make a release locally.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="after" class="doc-section">
    <h2>After the window opens</h2>
    <p>
      The UI is SolidJS under <code>apps/desktop/src</code>. Routes are one-line adapters in
      <code>pages/</code>. Product code is in <code>features/&lt;name&gt;/</code>. The viewport is
      <code>apps/desktop/src/viewport</code> (Three.js in <code>view3d/</code>, the 2D editor in
      <code>editor2d/</code>). The window talks to the sidecar over HTTP. It does not open the
      SQLite file.
    </p>
    <p>
      Geometry inside <code>packages/core</code> is millimetres and radians. Degrees stay in the UI
      or in the G-code post. If a dimension looks 57 times too large, a degree value crossed into
      core.
    </p>
    <p>
      Read <code>specs/00-overview.md</code> and <code>specs/01-conventions.md</code> before editing.
      Remaining work is ordered in <code>specs/roadmap/README.md</code> (RM-00–RM-11). The current
      step is RM-01, <code>specs/roadmap/01-action-catalogue.md</code>. The roadmap wins on sequence.
      Design specs win on contracts. Do not treat <code>specs/20-integral-roadmap.md</code> or
      <code>specs/21-integration-master-plan.md</code> as the queue.
    </p>
    <p>
      A work package edits its own directory. Cross-package work starts with a spec change and a
      change-log line. Code, comments, docs, and commit messages are English. Do not commit unless
      asked. Do not import <code>.reference/</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This docs site, from the same root:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="Astro docs (packages/web)">
{`bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web dev
bun run --filter @cadcamfun/web check`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>check</code> runs <code>packages/web/script/check-docs-index.ts</code>, which fails when
      a sidebar href has no index entry. It does not typecheck the desktop app.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="other-commands" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Other commands</h2>
    <p>Commands that are not in the blocks above.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Command</th><th>Where</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>bun run generate</code></td><td>Regenerate API clients.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>generate:check</code></td><td>Fail when generated clients are stale.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bun run --filter @cadcamfun/desktop dev</code></td><td>Vite webview without the Tauri shell.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bun src/main.ts</code></td><td>Sidecar start from <code>apps/sidecar</code>. Entry is <code>apps/sidecar/src/main.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>expo start --dev-client</code></td><td>Metro for the mobile development client.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>expo run:ios</code></td><td>Native iOS build from <code>apps/mobile</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bun test test</code></td><td>Mobile unit tests, run in <code>apps/mobile</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The webview entry is <code>apps/desktop/src/main.tsx</code>.</p>
  </section>
